{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794646699,"nonce":"HiwhBVqjrir5Z6k/w5kmau+iaEI=","message":"Android landed in 3d3fb0e5. The predicate the issue asked for is `RenderTree::animation_needs_full_paint`: a CSS animation or FLIP slide that a binding carries, and a CSS animation baked into a layer, leave the cached paint usable; a CSS animation or transition that is neither, one that lays out, a stopped one whose final values are still to be written, and a FLIP slide no binding carries, need a full paint. The Android loop asks it instead of raising a full paint on any CSS, transition or FLIP activity, and the desktop animate step uses it in place of its inline version. Transitions are still not carried (0623714), so a transition that is not baked still takes full paints.\n\niOS is not done. `tick_css` in ios.rs needs the same change; the file has uncommitted work in it, so I left it alone. The hunk:\n\n```\n-        if state_changed\n-            || active\n-            || !tree.css_transitions_empty()\n-            || tree.has_active_flip_animations()\n-        {\n+        // What a binding carries or a baked layer shows leaves the cached paint usable; the rest\n+        // of the animation work has the next frame paint in full.\n+        if state_changed || tree.animation_needs_full_paint() {\n             crate::mobile_paint::request_full_paint();\n         }\n```\n\nFound on the way: a FLIP binding was never released in a window, so the scheduler kept asking for frames at rest after every slide (9bf29c5, fixed in e24ec9cc). The runners would have inherited it.\n\nNot verified on a device. The Android crate builds for aarch64-linux-android. Remaining: the iOS hunk, and a device run that logs full against fast paints during a cn tabs reorder and a CSS-animated page (an iPhone is paired and available; the only Android device attached was an emulator).","files":null}]}